WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.793 --> 00:00:06.960
CHRIS GROSSMEIER: Hello,
my name's Chris Grossmeier,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.460
we'll be covering the Modeling
and Comparing Cloud Solutions

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.650
Costs and Strategy, Arc
213 session for Google.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.733
First, quick introduction.

00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:16.900
My name is Chris Grossmeier.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.990
I'm Senior Director of
product for Cloud Physics

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.730
in Santa Clara, California.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.950
I oversee the roadmap,
strategy, and the integration

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.220
of some the assessment tools
used by Google and the partners

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.840
to help customers
do assessments,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.925
costing, and planning.

00:00:29.925 --> 00:00:31.300
Our agenda for
today, we're going

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.685
to take a quick look at
analyzing your environment,

00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:35.560
we're going to see how
to assess and discover

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.670
your workloads, quantifying
your data, your TCO, and what's

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:43.540
on premise, and take a quick
dive into planning your Cloud

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:44.770
migration.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.260
And then, ultimately,
deploy and optimize.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.320
Following the basic Cloud
migration strategy from Google.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.540
So before we begin,
most important thing

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.140
I want to point out,
find a Cloud partner.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.270
Most customers are
only going to succeed

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.310
when they have a strategic
partner on board who

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.170
has done this multiple times.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.170
Take advantage of that partner,
learn from their experience,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.780
use their resources,
and train your staff up

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.250
with these solutions.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.890
Partners have been able
to do these services,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.055
have direct relationships
with Google.

00:01:16.055 --> 00:01:17.430
Take advantage of
their knowledge

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.590
and help that migration
go as smooth and as quick

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.715
the first time as possible.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.960
Also, if you're a partner,
take advantage of the funded

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.270
assessments from Google.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.930
If you're partner
or a user, you can

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.800
take advantage of requesting a
fully funded Google assessment

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.170
to analyze your
current infrastructure,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.950
do the matching, do
the TCO and analysis,

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.720
and find the best output
and solution for your Cloud

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.100
migration strategy.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.390
This can save weeks or months
for many organizations.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.860
So as a partner, you can sign
up at the Cloud Physics site

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:48.700
and register.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.610
And also for new users, you
can also request an assessment

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.097
through the customer portal or
from Cloud Physics directly.

00:01:55.097 --> 00:01:57.430
So as you begin and you start
working with your partner,

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.470
communicate why you're
interested in exploring

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.750
the Cloud for migration.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.982
Before you can really
advance on this journey,

00:02:03.982 --> 00:02:06.190
what is the reason that
you're getting into the Cloud

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.180
to begin with?

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.139
How will it be used?

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:12.040
Production, test, staging,
dev, we want to know

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.803
are the workloads
state for stateless

00:02:13.803 --> 00:02:15.220
and you can take
advantage of some

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.070
of the dynamics of the Cloud.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.120
Are you going to take
advantage of Cloud services

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.940
or do you have lots
of legacy applications

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:22.820
you're bringing over?

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.680
So are you re-platforming
or rebuilding?

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.010
Is there a compliance
implication you're focused on?

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.240
You're trying to keep
your workloads altogether

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.990
on dedicated hardware
in a secure environment.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:36.320
Or are you OK with going to
an instance based scenario?

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.240
Or do some workloads
actually really need

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.190
to stay within your
own data center?

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.810
As well as for
compliance, you need

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.660
to keep the patch management
and the resources up to date.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.730
Is there a business impact
of this cost, as well?

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.940
Are we going to gain a
OpEx or a CapEx savings?

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.292
Can we depreciate resources?

00:02:53.292 --> 00:02:55.500
Can we eliminate some of
the costs in the investments

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.420
that we have on premise?

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.150
Ultimately at the end,
which tier applications

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.700
are we bringing over there?

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.140
Do we need to run our most
mission critical application

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.360
in the Cloud to take
advantage of disaster recovery

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.970
and scalability or do we want
to take advantage of the Cloud

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.160
for elasticity for our
developers, or tests,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.170
and our staging
environments and keeping

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.760
our workloads contained
and controlled

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.302
by our own organization?

00:03:16.302 --> 00:03:17.760
So as we begin,
the very first step

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.800
in an assessment
in the GCP model

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.120
is analyze your environment.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.880
We want to do a
quick look of what

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.610
we're going to do and
sort of define an outcome.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.710
Like we mentioned, are
we moving to the Cloud

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.420
for a specific reason?

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.280
Are we trying to save money?

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.520
Are we trying to move
a specific outcome?

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.710
Are we trying to
re-platform an application?

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.170
Or just do an
application renewal?

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:41.970
By defining your outcome,
your stakeholders

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.762
have a clear compass
to know whether you're

00:03:43.762 --> 00:03:45.420
moving in the right
direction and which

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.950
choices you should focus on.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:51.790
Sole tenant, instances, Google
Cloud VMware Engine, services,

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:52.760
and so on.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.650
Those are all going to have
a very unique characteristic,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.793
and the outcome will really
help guide that scenario.

00:03:58.793 --> 00:04:00.960
As we begin, the financial
needs are the first place

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.310
we want to begin.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.920
On premise, we want to start
quantifying the resources

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.090
on our side first.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.640
We'll quantify the cost of
the Cloud a little bit later,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.760
but we need to know are we
looking at all of our resources

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.342
and what is the cost on premise.

00:04:13.342 --> 00:04:15.300
Is it going to allow us
to do comparisons later

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.649
in the lifecycle?

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:19.320
So on premise, I want
to build a TCO model.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.839
I want to quantify what
are my OpEx and CapEx

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.760
costs on premise?

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.720
And are we interested in doing a
Pay As You Go or On Demand type

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.895
model where we're paying
to keep resources on there

00:04:29.895 --> 00:04:31.770
and then we're going to
shut them on and off,

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.490
but not getting any
significant discounts?

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.630
Or are we going to commit
to a sustained usage

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.080
or committed use discount
for some of the workloads?

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.780
There is also costs
around migration.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.040
Are we going to have to
re-platform an application,

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.160
move it on to a new
operating system,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.850
and spend many man hours of
actually testing and migrating

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.720
workloads?

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.600
Or can we just do a quick
motion and bring that workload

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.850
to the Cloud, knowing
that's going to work exactly

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.190
as it did on premise?

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.980
Lastly, changes in the
infrastructure at the terms

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.180
of operating systems.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.210
If we own our own licenses,
can we bring our own licenses

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.260
to the Cloud?

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.200
Should we leverage standard
instances in the Cloud

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.850
available from the
Cloud provider?

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.070
Or should we go ahead and
leverage a free operating

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.700
system and change the model by
which we're using Oasis today?

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.100
As we begin, a key
part of this analysis

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.630
is to have a migration team.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.850
We wanted to find who
are the stakeholders,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:27.240
both at the line of business
and in IT and in the partner.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.310
Each of you will have an
impact on the direction

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.410
that we move the applications.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.360
We defined the
business stakeholders

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:35.830
and we wanted to define
the architecture team.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.640
So as we engage in application,
we know why we're moving it.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.690
Are we moving for
performance reasons?

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:43.590
Are we moving to take
advantage of certain platform?

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.493
Are we trying to get
closer to our customers?

00:05:46.493 --> 00:05:47.910
These people are
all going to have

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.490
a say and an input
on how we migrate

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.100
and why we make
migration decisions.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.130
So part of this TCO analysis
and the financial analysis

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:58.380
to look at what we have today.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.870
We want to understand what
is the quantity of resources

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.495
we have in our data center.

00:06:02.495 --> 00:06:03.870
What are our
servers and what are

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.780
the varying costs in age of
those resources that we've

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.380
invested in?

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.530
Do we have a
significant investment

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.380
in licenses for management,
things like multi-path,

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.530
things like operating systems,
things like virtualize storage

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.140
and virtualized network, or just
general enterprise management

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.100
as a whole?

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.070
Are we paying for additional
services on this platform?

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:25.720
Third party
applications that we're

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.370
using the Cloud for
today, and are those

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.215
going to be able to
carry over to the Cloud

00:06:30.215 --> 00:06:31.590
or are those things
that will end

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.900
when we look at the end of
our on premise infrastructure?

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:38.380
Also, quantifying the overhead
of our infrastructure, power

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.160
and cooling, square footage,
maybe even the cost of staff

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.740
if some of them are dedicated
purely to operational

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:46.640
use on premise.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.420
All of these we want to
quantify with TCO type analysis

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:53.488
and find out what is the cost
of our resources on premise.

00:06:53.488 --> 00:06:55.030
Once we've done
that, we can actually

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.160
take this even further,
some of the tools,

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:59.500
and actually find out what
is the cost per workload?

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.090
What is the cost per VM?

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.620
What is the cost
per hour, per day,

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:06.010
per year for an individual
workload in our data center?

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.480
And this is going
to be very useful

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.120
as we start modeling these
resources into the Cloud model.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.990
What metrics also matter is
going to be important for us.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.320
We want to go through and
look at all of our workloads

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.450
and determine is
there something unique

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.160
about the characteristics
on premise

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.760
that we're trying to fix.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.970
We want more performance
at the disk level.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.090
We want to eliminate
contention at the CPU.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.120
We want to eliminate memory
swapping and over-committed

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.080
hopes.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.440
These metrics that we monitor
on premise and collect data for

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.480
are something that we
won't use as the baseline.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.790
So as we get to
the Cloud later, we

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.010
can go back and see have
we actually improved

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:44.750
our performance and
actually improved

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.970
our experience for our users.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.700
By capturing these
resources today and seeing

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.620
where the bottlenecks
and the problems are,

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:54.752
we can monitor those metrics
when we get to the Cloud

00:07:54.752 --> 00:07:56.210
and do that comparison
and actually

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.230
quantify if we've
actually achieved

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:01.490
a significant improvement in our
availability, our performance,

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.140
or just the day
to day operations.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.710
So once you've done
the TCO analysis

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:10.570
on premise, the
next part of sort

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.393
of to start planning
your Cloud migration.

00:08:12.393 --> 00:08:14.560
We want to look at what are
the scenarios that we're

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.840
going to look at
and then use those

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.430
to build their financial costs.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:21.010
We can get into a comparison
a little bit later.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:22.510
So the first thing
to note, and this

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.380
is a good tip, not
everything can or should

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:27.890
move to the Cloud.

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.820
Filing print servers,
domain controllers.

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:33.970
Potentially if you have large,
hyper-converged infrastructure,

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:37.330
you may have infrastructure
for, such as storage arrays

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.190
and network components
that are virtualized

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:41.929
on premise that don't
need to move to the Cloud.

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:44.169
So take your time,
review the workflows

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:47.200
that you have on premise,
tag them, manage them,

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.850
and quantify which
ones you really

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.670
want to move to the Cloud,
which ones aren't necessary.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.410
You may find that
there's a large portion

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:54.490
of your investment.

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.400
Powered off VMs, resources
that are dedicated to

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.460
on premise resources that
don't need to go to the Cloud

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.440
and can be a significant
cost savings.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.920
So focus on what
you have locally,

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.750
work on those
resources, tag them,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.090
and we'll use those for
filtering and reducing

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:13.550
our scope a bit later.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:14.670
So our workload analysis.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.810
A key part of what we have
on premise is each workload

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:19.460
has a defined size.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:21.600
Every application owner
has come to you and said,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.390
I want 16 or 32 processors,
I want 64 gigs of RAM,

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:27.920
I want all the storage.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.695
Obviously, they have a business
need for their request.

00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:34.470
But the reality is they may not
have ever used those resources.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:35.845
Sort of right
sizing the workload

00:09:35.845 --> 00:09:37.760
is going to be very key.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.400
Giving the users the
opportunity to reassess

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.650
what is the ideal
size of that workload

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:45.820
can have a significant cost
savings for us in the Cloud.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.410
There's a reason to pay for
32 CPU's when the workload has

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:51.830
never used more than four or
eight CPU's in the on premise

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:53.250
environment.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.070
In addition, we want
to map these workloads

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:56.270
to different instances.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:59.120
As we start doing analysis, the
tools that we want to leverage

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.340
today we want to
find every workload

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:03.650
and find out what is
the ideal workload

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.110
in that new environment.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.220
Right sizing it first
and then mapping it

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:08.850
into those resources.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:12.630
So we may find that instead of
16, we can get by eight CPU's.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:15.830
Instead of 64 gigs of RAM,
we could get by at 24.

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:18.440
By mapping these instances
after the right sizing,

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:22.520
we can now quantify a cost for
each one of those base instance

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:24.410
types.

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:28.250
Storage has an additional level
of optimization all to its own.

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.550
Many workloads on premise have
performance characteristics

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.680
that are limited by the array.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.520
In the Cloud, we can choose SSD
or hard drive or even optimal.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.100
So based on the IOPS
and the throughput

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.690
of that specific
application, we may

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.638
recommend that it go to an SSD.

00:10:43.638 --> 00:10:45.680
But the volume is large
enough or the performance

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:49.400
is low enough, maybe we can
put it onto a hard disk drive.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:51.590
Using the tools and knowing
what the metrics are

00:10:51.590 --> 00:10:54.260
for each workload allows
us to dynamically adjust

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.170
these on the fly.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.990
In the end result,
we'll have a cost that's

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:00.850
optimized for each workload.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:02.350
Lastly, there's
some choices that we

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.890
need to make around how we
want to put these on the Cloud.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:07.870
Traditionally, we'd
always put resources out

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.360
there as instances
in the Google Cloud

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:14.080
where every operating system
got its own instance on a shared

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:15.880
resource in the infrastructure.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:18.310
But with the event
of sole tenant nodes,

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:21.430
we can move our own workloads
onto a dedicated host which

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:24.220
is only occupied by
our own organization

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:26.100
and not shared
with anybody else.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:29.200
To advantage a sole tenant
nodes also is over subscription.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.920
So what if I have a lot of
idle or lightly used workloads

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:33.880
throughout the month?

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:36.940
I can oversubscribe this host,
potentially up to four to one

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:39.370
and gain the benefit
of over subscription

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:42.770
that I couldn't do with
individual instances.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:44.650
And lastly, the
newly released Google

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:49.300
Cloud VMware Engine, allowing
us to run workloads in VMware

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.910
on Google Cloud as
a dedicated node.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:54.610
Big cost savings to move
workloads just with the vMotion

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:57.040
from on premise to the
Cloud without having

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.130
to go through any
re-platforming or any migration.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:03.530
Lastly, what are your
deciding factors?

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:04.210
Is it time?

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.780
Do I need to be on the
Cloud as quick as possible?

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:08.680
Is it I need to refresh?

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:10.540
Or am I re-platforming
an application

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:12.677
and modernizing my applications?

00:12:12.677 --> 00:12:15.010
All these are going to be
important factors for workload

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:15.700
analysis.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:20.020
As we start modeling,
we want to start

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:21.780
looking at the
characteristics that

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.700
are going to have an
impact for each instance.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:28.210
Choosing a geography will have
an impact on cost immediately.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.980
So knowing that I have a
specific geography in mind,

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.880
I can move it to wherever I want
for the maximum cost savings

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.100
or have it as close to an
end user community that

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:38.520
uses an application.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.310
I can explore that sole
tenant node option,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:43.420
taking advantage
of dedicated hosts.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:44.970
And with dedicated
hosts, the ability

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.610
to bring my own Microsoft
licenses to the Cloud

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:50.990
and take advantage of my
own software assurance.

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:52.520
If you're a VMware
customer today

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:58.190
and you have a GVCE Google
Cloud Virtual VMware engine

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.695
option available to you,
move those workloads directly

00:13:00.695 --> 00:13:02.570
into VMware in the Cloud
and potentially have

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:06.937
a significant savings in time
and cost of infrastructure.

00:13:06.937 --> 00:13:08.520
But importantly, we
want to understand

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.740
what our commitments are.

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:13.590
As we commit to those
resources and time,

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:17.340
On Demand or one year
or three year commits,

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.800
we will achieve different
levels of discount

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:22.230
for each one of these solutions.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.770
Lastly, understand the skills
and capability of your team,

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:26.660
as well.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:28.230
So as I run through
this simulation

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:30.675
and I'm mapping my
workloads to the Cloud,

00:13:30.675 --> 00:13:32.850
if I'm planning to move
them into instances

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.950
or I'm planning to
move them to services,

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:37.740
is my team or my partner
capable of doing that?

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:40.710
Or will I gain the advantage of
just vMotioning these workloads

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:43.590
into the Cloud and then learning
that skill as we go through

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.422
them one at a time,
but gain the advantage

00:13:46.422 --> 00:13:48.380
of getting on the Cloud
as quickly as possible?

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:52.000
So big choice.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.010
Jump all in or go hybrid.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.620
If I jump all in, am I
going to do one fell swoop

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:58.450
and bring it all to the Cloud?

00:13:58.450 --> 00:13:59.920
Or can I run in a
hybrid mode where

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.500
I bring specific applications
to the Cloud one at a time,

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:06.400
keeping some of my resources on
premise and my own data center

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.490
and run those in the Cloud
as I bring them over.

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:12.880
I need to understand what needs
to stay and what needs to go.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:14.890
Understand the relationship
to these resources

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:16.190
is also important.

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:18.340
If lots of traffic
between two VM's,

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:21.010
I want to ensure that I bring
all the relevant resources

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.010
with me to the Cloud
at the same time.

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:26.190
So this is where we get
into discussion of what

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:29.310
options and impact each has.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:31.440
If we adopt Google
Cloud Services,

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.500
we can see a significant savings
by taking out the operating

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.460
system in the instant causes and
focusing solely on the service.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.020
So adopting Google
Cloud Services

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:44.040
for cloud SQL, SAP on
Google, Kubernetes,

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.250
any of the advanced machine
learning engines and resources

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:50.070
can be a significant cost
savings over running instances

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.840
for years on an
organization, giving you

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.030
true dynamic capabilities.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:56.520
But I may have to do
some lift and shift.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.300
I may have applications that
are legacy applications, Windows

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:03.840
2003, and those resources that
I want to bring to the Cloud.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:07.230
So do I bring those applications
onto new OS instances

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:08.130
on the Cloud?

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.050
Or do I just migrate
that workload?

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:11.580
Things like
Velostrata streamline

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.750
that automation for us.

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:17.020
Or quickly migrate with the
Google Cloud VMware engine.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.300
If I have a legacy
infrastructure today

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.430
and time is of the
essence, quickly

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:24.010
V motioning those
workloads over to the Cloud

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.620
may offer a quick advantage,
but ultimately may

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:29.170
mean some of these
applications remain around

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:30.550
much longer than you intend.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:33.220
Ease of bringing it to the
Cloud doesn't necessarily

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:37.728
mean that it's going to end
up getting re-platformed.

00:15:37.728 --> 00:15:39.270
When we look at the
instance options,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:40.920
we want to look at
all the factors that

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.140
impact our instance choices.

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:47.280
Talked about right sizing, and
do we optimize those resources.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:50.220
Knowing that an application
is multi-threaded and takes

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:53.550
advantage of multiple
CPU's, right sizing it down

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.583
may actually have an
impact on its performance.

00:15:55.583 --> 00:15:58.000
So we definitely want to engage
with the lines of business

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.770
and the application
owners around this.

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.900
Optimizing storage
for disk type.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:07.280
If we are bandwidth constrained
at the disk level, we're IO,

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:10.640
we're in throughput constrained,
taking advantage of SSDs

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:12.740
in the Cloud may give us
a significant performance

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:13.610
increase.

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:16.520
Or maybe changing the
storage model altogether.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:19.520
Dev test, QA, all the
different staging,

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.950
maybe they don't need
to be on full instances.

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:24.980
Moving them to On Demand
or pre-emptive instances

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:26.900
could offer significant
savings for us

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:30.590
and have a big impact on
the long term strategy.

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:32.720
Lastly, choosing the
right instance class

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.970
is something we want to
start with right away.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.980
Focusing on what
is it that we need.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:39.650
Do we need an Intel processor
for the Intel architecture

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:41.090
and the Intel commands?

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:44.363
Am I going to go with the latest
and fastest Intel processor?

00:16:44.363 --> 00:16:46.280
Am I going to go with
something where I really

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:48.322
don't know the underlying
infrastructure for cost

00:16:48.322 --> 00:16:50.180
savings, like an E2 instance?

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.483
Or maybe I have a commitment
to an AMD solution

00:16:52.483 --> 00:16:53.900
that's in the
infrastructure and I

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.360
know that I have an
AMD processor need.

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:59.300
Choosing each of these will
have a financial impact cost.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.700
We'll reveal that through the
tools and the calculations.

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:07.410
Sole Tenant Nodes versus
Google Cloud VMware Engine.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:11.200
A strategic choice here is that
I can over-commit Sole Tenant

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:12.700
Nodes and take
advantage of bringing

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:14.890
my own licenses over instances.

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:16.863
I can do the same with
the VMware Engine.

00:17:16.863 --> 00:17:19.030
But with the VMware Engine,
I can leverage my VMware

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:20.859
architecture as
it is today, take

00:17:20.859 --> 00:17:22.880
advantage of the VMware tools.

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:24.550
But the Google Cloud
is going to allow

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:27.670
me to do some functionalities
very specifically to the Cloud

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:30.910
without paying for the addition
of the hypervisor overhead.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.395
If I'm not going to need
VMware in the Cloud,

00:17:33.395 --> 00:17:35.770
maybe I don't need to bring
it to the Google Cloud VMware

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:37.660
Engine.

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:42.160
One of the key things about
Sole Tenant Nodes and the GCVE

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.620
is that I can bring my own
licenses for the Microsoft

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:45.510
solution.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.980
Or I could always bring my
licenses for Linux solutions

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:49.180
to instances.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:50.920
The limitations on
Microsoft licensing

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:52.600
allows me to bring
those licenses

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:54.360
to those dedicated OS models.

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.840
So big discussion options
around optimization

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.260
and the impact of each.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:04.760
So if we're willing to
commit to resources,

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:08.360
we want to take advantage of
the discounts we can achieve.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:10.527
If we commit to a
On Demand instance

00:18:10.527 --> 00:18:12.360
and the workloads are
going to be out a lot,

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:14.527
we want to leave them on
as much as possible to gain

00:18:14.527 --> 00:18:16.670
that sustained usage discounts.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:19.370
On Demand and pre-emptive,
I will gain that advantage

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:21.090
for sustained use.

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:23.690
But if I'm willing to commit
to a year or three year,

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:26.450
I can get even greater savings
with that committed use

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:27.540
discount.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:29.450
This is going to
apply to all instances

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:31.890
and convert them into regular
instances on the instance

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:33.260
model.

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:36.848
For the Sole Tenant
Node and the GCVE,

00:18:36.848 --> 00:18:38.390
it's committing to
the infrastructure

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:41.910
over one year or three year
to maximize that savings.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:43.590
So as I go through
these calculations,

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:45.235
I want to know
all these factors.

00:18:45.235 --> 00:18:46.610
Could I match the
right resource?

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:47.902
Did I choose the right storage?

00:18:47.902 --> 00:18:49.700
Did I choose the
right commitment?

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.650
Did I choose the
right geography?

00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:55.220
All those are going to play
into our scenario here.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:57.160
So once we've gone
through and played

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.032
with some of the
assessment tools,

00:18:59.032 --> 00:19:00.490
you're going to
have the capability

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:01.660
of doing comparisons.

00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:02.980
We did that early TCO.

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:05.350
We modeled what our
cost was on premise.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:07.890
We're actually able to
find that cost per VM.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:10.210
We're actually able to do
that same modeling then

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:11.960
against the Cloud instance.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:13.780
What was that cost
of that single VM

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:17.620
in the Cloud on a Sole Tenant
Node or as an instance?

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:19.150
Know your current
support services

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:20.770
versus future support services.

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:23.210
If you have resources
and people on site

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:25.233
and you're paying
for those on premise,

00:19:25.233 --> 00:19:26.650
how are those going
to be impacted

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:27.832
if you can cut those costs?

00:19:27.832 --> 00:19:30.040
Or are you going to be
picking up additional services

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:31.890
on the Cloud side?

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.550
Things like security,
logging, load balancing,

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:38.220
different concepts of the
infrastructure, certificate

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.200
management, and
support all are going

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:43.740
to play into your cost
model comparisons.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:47.790
Lastly, we want to plan for
the disaster recovery scenario.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:50.130
Fortunately for us, Google
takes care of the hardware

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:51.415
infrastructure for us.

00:19:51.415 --> 00:19:53.040
Should a host fail,
it would bring back

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:54.270
the resources for us.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:57.600
So I don't need to build
an N+1 or N+2 architecture.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:01.050
But I do want to plan for
corruption and loss of data.

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:04.330
So planning for a storage and
archiving and backup scenario

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:08.083
is going to be key as we
get to the Cloud, as well.

00:20:08.083 --> 00:20:10.250
Once we've done all this,
we'll collect all the data

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:11.642
through the assessment models.

00:20:11.642 --> 00:20:13.850
This is where a tool, like
the Cloud Physics Analysis

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:15.270
is going to be critical.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:17.020
We're going to bring
all of our content in

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:18.562
and we're going to
start prioritizing

00:20:18.562 --> 00:20:20.150
our migration of components.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:23.940
Now, key tip here, do I move
my most critical application

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:24.560
first?

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:26.150
Move the low hanging fruit.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.830
This should be an obvious one.

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:30.440
We want the team to learn by
moving some of the resources

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:31.280
first.

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:34.100
Let them test and learn
how the tools work

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:35.540
and how they respond.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:38.630
Learn for compatibility,
learn for build restrictions

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:41.180
and different builds
and versions that

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:44.000
are out there that may not be
compatible with each other.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.610
Let them learn on the platform
by bringing that low hanging

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:48.320
fruit, the test
environment, the dev

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:51.650
environment, before you commit
to the larger enterprise.

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:54.110
Hopefully, a strategic partner
if they're involved with you

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:56.570
can give you a lot of
heads up to a lot of these.

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:59.540
But ultimately, you
know your applications,

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:02.735
you know the limits and the
requirements, and testing it

00:21:02.735 --> 00:21:05.350
is going to be really critical
for your team to focus on

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:08.480
in the early stages.

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.560
So our last big
portion of the planning

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:14.290
is going to be deployed
optimize in the Google model.

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:17.200
So where we can, we want
to use the services.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.758
If we have mail and
exchange on premise,

00:21:19.758 --> 00:21:21.550
maybe we can take
advantage of Google Mail.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.800
If we have exchange
servers on premise,

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:25.290
maybe we can use Cloud SQL.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.040
We can take advantage
of the services that

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:30.770
make the most sense if we
can achieve a cost savings.

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:33.280
In many cases, if it's an
instance today on premise

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:35.560
and you can bring
it to the Cloud,

00:21:35.560 --> 00:21:39.610
we can find a similar service
that has reduced cost.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.950
We want to monitor
what you are consuming.

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.210
What are you using on premise?

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:44.590
What are you using in the Cloud?

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:46.510
And what was used for
certain portions of it?

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:49.330
Because your utilization
during migration or during peak

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:52.790
usage may be significantly
different throughout the year.

00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:55.030
So if you're doing an
assessment in the off season,

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.250
understand what
you're consuming.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:00.160
Optimize your resources,
that makes sense.

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:03.670
For your small dev tests in
QA instances or developer

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:07.270
desktops, making them smaller
because they're idle is fine.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:09.220
But for an application
that takes advantage

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:12.370
that multi-threaded
environment has lots of cores,

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:16.010
maybe optimization isn't
critical as availability

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:17.150
and performance.

00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:19.900
So understand what the business
need before you right size

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:23.150
or optimize those resources.

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:26.280
A big portion of this is
Cloud Service Integration.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.350
If I can integrate
to a Cloud service?

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:30.000
Or do I want it to
be Cloud agnostic

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.480
and bring my own OS instances,
my own applications?

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.230
The tighter I get
coupled into the Cloud,

00:22:35.230 --> 00:22:37.090
the more savings I may
be able to achieve,

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:38.836
but the harder it
is to move later.

00:22:38.836 --> 00:22:42.520
And ultimately,
Kubernetes and containers,

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:45.070
if I'm going stateless or
I have a lot of resources

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:47.770
that are very dynamic
being traded on the fly,

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:50.590
take advantage of the new
technologies for Kubernetes.

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:53.020
Allow your environment
to use things like Anthos

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:56.800
and leverage the container
model to really save resources

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:59.355
as we move.

00:22:59.355 --> 00:23:01.690
So an optimization
decision points here.

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:04.500
So does your staff have
the skill set now required

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:06.000
for Cloud management?

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.160
If they were strong
in the VMware tools,

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.310
maybe the VMware tools on
the Cloud makes more sense.

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:14.550
If they're really just strong
on their core applications

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:16.950
and the infrastructure was
not their strong point,

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:21.500
Cloud migration may be a very
simple choice for you to make.

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:23.990
The cost of migration,
lifting and shifting

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:26.750
resources to the
Cloud or running tools

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:30.200
to do a VM migration
can be very costly

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:32.990
versus just doing something
as simple as a vMotion.

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:36.440
Right sizing and re-platform
is key to the solution,

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:39.440
or application modernization
is key to the solution.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:41.540
You're going to incur
more cost upfront.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.910
But a vMotion will
get me on the Cloud

00:23:43.910 --> 00:23:45.440
and get me over there quicker.

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:47.960
Then I can focus on the
resources in the Cloud

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.060
and not pay for that
inner region bandwidth

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:52.285
type scenarios.

00:23:52.285 --> 00:23:54.160
Does your cost benefit
from Sole Tenant Nodes

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:55.330
or shared infrastructure?

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:57.190
The more I can
oversubscribed, the more

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:59.770
I can commit my
resources the better.

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:02.770
If I can oversubscribed my host
because a lot of my workloads

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:06.380
are relatively idle, that's
a big cost advantage.

00:24:06.380 --> 00:24:09.640
But if my workloads are
one to one for CPU's and I

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:13.420
want to guarantee a commitment,
again, Sole Tenant Nodes

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:14.890
may offer an
advantage that I can

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:16.840
have my own resource,
my own host,

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:18.690
not shared with anybody else.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:20.290
But the costs of
shared infrastructure

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:23.116
may be a negative in that case.

00:24:23.116 --> 00:24:24.780
And again, operating systems.

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:26.265
We've touched on
this many times.

00:24:26.265 --> 00:24:28.890
There is a significant advantage
to bringing your own operating

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:29.550
systems.

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:32.760
If you've already invested
in software assurance and you

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:36.420
have a multi-year agreement
with a software manufacturer

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:38.910
or a license manager for
the operating systems.

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.040
Bringing your own license
to the Cloud guarantees

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:42.480
your investment is not lost.

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:46.080
It allows you to bring that
resource over and reuse it.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:48.060
If you own Linux,
you can do BYOL

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:51.120
across all instances
and all solutions.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:53.820
But BYOL for Windows
environment only

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:57.600
applies to the Sole Tenant Nodes
and the Google Cloud VMware

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:58.950
Engine.

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:01.050
Last big choice, Will Free Do.

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.453
You may find that a lot of
the free operating systems

00:25:03.453 --> 00:25:05.370
may be sufficient for
what you're looking for.

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:07.580
If I don't need an enterprise
class redhead instance

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:09.960
and I can get by with
a free Linux version,

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:14.960
it may be sufficient for some of
your non-critical applications.

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:16.600
So some final thoughts.

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:20.270
Plan, test, migrate,
learn, repeat.

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:23.200
This is going to be
key to the success.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:24.550
Leverage that partner.

00:25:24.550 --> 00:25:26.710
Go through these
steps multiple times.

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:28.450
Start with the
low hanging fruit.

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:30.520
Bring those resources
over the Cloud.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:31.960
Learn what works and doesn't.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:33.158
And then repeat and correct.

00:25:33.158 --> 00:25:35.200
It's going to be an
important factor for bringing

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.970
these resources over.

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:40.040
So at that, I'm going
to say thank you.

00:25:40.040 --> 00:25:42.380
Quick final note though,
there are resources

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:44.240
available from the Google team.

